{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pon-08-dividing-a-total-into-parts",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Dividing a Total into Parts",
    "description": "Split a known total into two, three or four parts by turning weighted, reciprocal, percentage and equal-result clues into ratios or equations.",
    "teachingApproach": "Start from the whole: two parts become x and Total−x. Then show when reciprocal conditions create a quadratic, when equal fractional parts should be converted straight into a ratio, how chained ratios share a common middle term, and how different transformations can hide one common value. Add successive-percentage remainder stories and finish with a representation-choice capstone.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "Teach partition problems with a clear whole-first model. For two parts use x and Total−x unless a ratio is faster. Cover weighted sums such as 10 times one part plus 22 times the other, reciprocal-sum conditions, three-part equal-fraction conditions that convert directly to a ratio, chained ratios, and four parts that become equal after different operations. Include percentage-removal stories where each percentage acts on the current remainder. Finish with a capstone where the learner must choose between a ratio setup and a direct equation."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's split totals without inventing more variables than we need.\n\nThe whole is your anchor. From there, a part can be described by subtraction, a ratio, a reciprocal condition or a chain of equal transformed values.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When a known total is split into two parts, one variable is enough.\n\nIf the total is 60 and the first part is x, the second part is 60−x.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Suppose 3 times the first plus 5 times the second equals 252:\n3x + 5(60−x)=252\n3x+300−5x=252\n2x=48\nx=24\n\nThe parts are 24 and 36.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
